# Spinach: The 40-Day Leaf Crop That Sells Every Week

First, a clarification that saves money: in Kenyan markets, "spinach" usually means **Swiss chard** (the broad-leafed green with thick stalks), while true spinach is the smaller, crinkly-leaved crop. Buyers and hotel kitchens use both names loosely — so before you plant, confirm *which* green your buyer means. This guide covers both, because the plan is nearly identical and the distinction is the whole market game.

## Where both crops grow well

- **Altitude:** 1,200–2,600 m
- **Rainfall:** 500–1,000 mm, or irrigation
- **Temperature:** 15–24 °C — heat makes both bolt (flower early)
- **Soil:** rich loam, pH 6.0–7.5, heavily composted

Cool highlands grow them year-round; that's why leafy greens are the steady-income crops of Meru's upper zones.

## Pick the right variety — for the right buyer

**Swiss chard — Fordhook Giant** — the market's default "spinach": thick stalks, huge leaves, 60 days to first pick, then **re-harvested weekly for months**. The hotel-and-greengrocer standard.

**True spinach — Bloomsdale Longstanding** — crinkly dark leaves, 35–45 days to first pick, slower to bolt than most. The choice where buyers pay per kg for genuine spinach.

Seed rate: **2–3 kg per acre** (chard) or **3–4 kg per acre** (true spinach, denser), direct-sown or transplanted at **30 cm × 20 cm**.

## The timeline that matters

- **Week −1:** work in well-rotted manure or compost — leafy crops live on soil fertility.
- **Week 0 (sowing):** direct-sow 1 cm deep at the first reliable rains, or transplant 3–4-week seedlings. Basal: **50 kg DAP per acre**.
- **Week 2:** first weeding — these crops have zero patience for weed competition.
- **Week 3:** first top-dress — **50 kg CAN per acre**. Leaf crops are nitrogen engines: repeat at week 5 and week 7 (true spinach), or every 3–4 weeks through the season (chard).
- **Week 5–6 (first pick, true spinach):** cut outer leaves, leave the crown — the plant re-grows for 2–3 more cuts. Chard's first pick comes ~week 9, then weekly.
- **Every week after:** pick in the cool morning, bunch immediately, and sell **the same day** — these leaves do not wait.

Panda's season planner tracks the cuts and top-dresses on your phone, in English and Swahili.

## Pests and diseases that cost leaf farmers money

1. **Aphids** — curl the new growth; check the undersides of young leaves. Squash early colonies, and only spray registered products if they spread.
2. **Leaf miners** — silvery tunnels through the leaf; remove and destroy mined leaves, and use registered products only at threshold — tunnels cost appearance, not the whole crop.
3. **Downy mildew** — grey fuzz under leaves in damp spells; wide spacing, morning watering, and registered downy-active sprays at first specks.
4. **Cercospora leaf spot** — round grey-brown spots with dark borders; clean seed and rotation help more than late sprays.

Advisory note: Panda's treatment guidance is advisory — always use PCPB-registered products at label rates and observe pre-harvest intervals. When in doubt, your local extension officer sees the same photo you do.

## Sell same-day, every week — the subscription crop

Prices run roughly **40–80 KES per kg** (or **20–50 KES per bunch**) at retail in Meru and Kirinyaga, steadier than almost any other crop because demand — hotels, hospitals, households — never pauses. The business model is different from cabbage: instead of one big harvest, you get a **weekly payroll** for the life of the stand. That's the trade: modest margins, relentless timing. Panda's price checks cover both greens weekly in season.

## The bottom line

Input bill: **10,000–15,000 KES per acre**. Yield: **4–8 tonnes per acre** across multiple cuts. One acre of chard, sold weekly at 40+ KES per kg, pays a labourer's wage every single week the stand lives. It's the closest crop in this catalogue to a salary.

